<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Protest and freedom · 1921 · South &amp; Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="sg-title">Sarfaroshi Ki Tamanna</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>An Urdu poem of 1921 that young fighters for India's freedom from British rule took up as their call.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Bismil Azimabadi</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>the Indian independence movement</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Urdu</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>British India</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>1921</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Protest and freedom</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (words credited to Bismil Azimabadi (published 1921): in the public domain in the United States, published before 1930; they may still be protected in India)</dd></div></dl>
<p class="kd-note">This song is about hard things from the past, like war, cruelty or death. You can read about it with a grown-up.</p><button type="button" class="kd-story sg-kd-open">Read about this song</button>
<div class="sg-body sg-fold">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarfaroshi-ki-tamanna">Sarfaroshi Ki Tamanna</a>&quot; (&quot;the desire for sacrifice&quot;) is an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urdu">Urdu</a> patriotic poem written by Bismil Azimabadi as a dedication to the young freedom fighters of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-independence">Indian independence</a> movement. It was written in 1921, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jallianwala-bagh-massacre">Jallianwala Bagh massacre</a> and other atrocities by the British colonial rulers, and first published in the journal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sabah">Sabah</a>, published from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>. It is a ghazal of 11 couplets. The Khuda Bakhsh Library has preserved the original copy and the page of the poet's diary holding the poem, with corrections by his mentor, Shad Azimabadi.</p><p>The poem was popularised by Ram Prasad Bismil, an Indian freedom fighter, who made it a war cry against British rule; when he was put on the gallows, its opening lines were on his lips. It has also been associated with the younger generation of freedom fighters between the wars, among them Ashfaqullah Khan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhagat-singh">Bhagat Singh</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chandra-shekhar-azad">Chandrashekhar Azad</a>.</p><p>It has kept its place in public life. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manmohan-singh">Manmohan Singh</a> recited its first line in his Budget Speech of 1992 in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lok-sabha">Lok Sabha</a>, and in recent years it has been taken up by mass movements such as the anti-CAA protests in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and the Pakistani Students <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solidarity-polish-trade-union">Solidarity</a> March. It has been used in many Hindi films, from Manoj Kumar's Shaheed (1965), on the life of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhagat-singh">Bhagat Singh</a>, to Rang De Basanti (2006) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhuj">Bhuj</a>: The Pride of India (2021).</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A poem of readiness for sacrifice in the cause of freedom, dedicated to the young people who fought for it. The mood is fierce and unafraid.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Urdu</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="rom">Romanized</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-3"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Urdu</span><span class="sg-r">Romanized</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ur" dir="rtl">سرفروشی کی تمنا اب ہمارے دل میں ہے</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ur-Latn">sarfaroshī kī tamannā ab hamāre dil meṉ hai</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The longing to give our heads is now in our heart;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ur" dir="rtl">دیکھنا ہے زور کتنا بازوئے قاتل میں ہے</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ur-Latn">dekhnā hai zor kitnā bāzū-yi qātil meṉ hai</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>we shall see how much strength is in the arm of the killer.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ur" dir="rtl">رہ روئے راہِ محبت! رہ نہ جانا راہ میں</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ur-Latn">rahrav-i rāh-i muhabbat! rah na jānā rāh meṉ</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Traveller on the road of love! Do not stay behind on the road:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ur" dir="rtl">لذتِ صحرا نوردی دوری منزل میں ہے</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ur-Latn">lazzat-i sahrā-navardī dūrī-yi manzil meṉ hai</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the pleasure of wandering the desert lies in the distance of the goal.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ur" dir="rtl">سرفروشی کی تمنا اب ہمارے دل میں ہے</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ur-Latn">sarfaroshī kī tamannā ab hamāre dil meṉ hai</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The longing to give our heads is now in our heart.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ur" dir="rtl">کرتا نہیں کیوں دوسرا کچھ بات چیت</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ur-Latn">kartā nahīṉ kyoṉ dūsrā kuchh bāt-chīt</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Why does no one else say anything at all?</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ur" dir="rtl">دیکھتا ہوں میں جسے وہ چپ تیری محفل میں ہے</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ur-Latn">dekhtā hūṉ maiṉ jise vuh chup terī mahfil meṉ hai</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Whoever I look at is silent in your gathering.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ur" dir="rtl">اے شہید ملک و ملت تیرے اوپر نثار</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ur-Latn">ai shahīd-i mulk o millat tere ūpar nisār</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>O martyr for country and community, [we are] laid down for you:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ur" dir="rtl">تیری قربانی کا چرچا غیر کی محفل میں ہے</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ur-Latn">terī qurbānī kā charchā ghair kī mahfil meṉ hai</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>your sacrifice is talked of in the gathering of strangers.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ur" dir="rtl">سرفروشی کی تمنا اب ہمارے دل میں ہے</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ur-Latn">sarfaroshī kī tamannā ab hamāre dil meṉ hai</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The longing to give our heads is now in our heart.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ur" dir="rtl">وقت آنے دے بتا دیں گے تجھے اے آسماں</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ur-Latn">vaqt āne de batā deṉ ge tujhe ai āsmāṉ</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Let the time come, we will tell you, O heaven;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ur" dir="rtl">ہم ابھی سے کیا بتائیں کیا ہمارے دل میں ہے</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ur-Latn">ham abhī se kyā batāʾeṉ kyā hamāre dil meṉ hai</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>what should we tell now of what is in our heart?</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ur" dir="rtl">کھینج کر لائی ہے سب کو قتل ہونے کی امید</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ur-Latn">khīṉch kar lāʾī hai sab ko qatl hone kī ummīd</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The hope of being killed has drawn everyone here:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ur" dir="rtl">عاشقوں کا آج جمگھٹ کوچہ قاتل میں ہے</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ur-Latn">ʿāshiqoṉ kā āj jamghaṭ kūchah-i qātil meṉ hai</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>today the lovers throng in the killer's lane.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ur" dir="rtl">سرفروشی کی تمنا اب ہمارے دل میں ہے</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ur-Latn">sarfaroshī kī tamannā ab hamāre dil meṉ hai</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The longing to give our heads is now in our heart.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ur" dir="rtl">ہے لیے ہتھیار دشمن تاک میں بیٹھا ادھر</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ur-Latn">hai liye hathyār dushman tāk meṉ baiṭhā udhar</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The enemy sits in wait over there, weapons in hand,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ur" dir="rtl">اور ہم تیار ہیں سینہ لیے اپنا ادھر</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ur-Latn">aur ham taiyār haiṉ sīnah liye apnā idhar</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and we are ready here, offering our own chests.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ur" dir="rtl">خون سے کھیلیں گے ہولی گر وطن مشکل میں ہے</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ur-Latn">khūn se khelen ge holī gar vatan mushkil meṉ hai</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>We will play Holi with blood if the homeland is in trouble.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ur" dir="rtl">سرفروشی کی تمنا اب ہمارے دل میں ہے</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ur-Latn">sarfaroshī kī tamannā ab hamāre dil meṉ hai</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The longing to give our heads is now in our heart.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ur" dir="rtl">ہاتھ جن میں ہو جنون کٹتے نہیں تلوار سے</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ur-Latn">hāth jin meṉ ho junūn kaṭte nahīṉ talvār se</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Hands that hold passion are not cut off by the sword;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ur" dir="rtl">سر جو اٹھ جاتے ہیں وہ جھکتے نہیں للکا ر سے</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ur-Latn">sar jo uṭh jāte haiṉ vuh jhukte nahīṉ lalkār se</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>heads that are raised do not bow at a challenge.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ur" dir="rtl">اور بھڑکے گا جو شعلہ سا ہمارے دل میں ہے</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ur-Latn">aur bhaṛke gā jo shuʿlah sā hamāre dil meṉ hai</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>What is like a flame in our heart will blaze up more.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ur" dir="rtl">سرفروشی کی تمنا اب ہمارے دل میں ہے</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ur-Latn">sarfaroshī kī tamannā ab hamāre dil meṉ hai</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The longing to give our heads is now in our heart.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ur" dir="rtl">ہم جو گھر سے نکلے ہی تھے باندھ کے سر پہ کفن</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ur-Latn">ham jo ghar se nikle hī the bāṉdh ke sar pah kafan</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>We, who had only just left home with the shroud tied on our heads,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ur" dir="rtl">جان ہتھیلی پر لیے لو، لے چلے ہیں یہ قدم</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ur-Latn">jān hathelī par liye lo, le chale haiṉ yih qadam</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>carrying our lives on our palms, look, we have set out on these steps:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ur" dir="rtl">زندگی تو اپنی مہمان موت کی محفل میں ہے</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ur-Latn">zindagī to apnī mahmān maut kī mahfil meṉ hai</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>our life is a guest in the gathering of death.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ur" dir="rtl">سرفروشی کی تمنا اب ہمارے دل میں ہے</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ur-Latn">sarfaroshī kī tamannā ab hamāre dil meṉ hai</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The longing to give our heads is now in our heart.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ur" dir="rtl">یوں کھڑا مقتل میں قاتل کہہ رہا ہے بار بار</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ur-Latn">yūṉ khaṛā maqtal meṉ qātil kah rahā hai bār bār</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>So the killer, standing at the place of slaughter, says again and again:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ur" dir="rtl">کیا تمناِئے شہادت بھی کِسی کے دِل میں ہے</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ur-Latn">kyā tamannā-yi shahādat bhī kisī ke dil meṉ hai</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>'Is there a longing for martyrdom in anyone's heart, too?'</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ur" dir="rtl">دل میں طوفانوں کی ٹولی اور نسوں میں انقلاب</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ur-Latn">dil meṉ tūfānoṉ kī ṭolī aur nasoṉ meṉ inqilāb</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>A band of storms in the heart, and revolution in the veins:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ur" dir="rtl">ہوش دشمن کے اڑا دیں گے ہمیں روکو نہ آج</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ur-Latn">hosh dushman ke uṛā deṉ ge hameṉ roko na āj</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>we will blow away the enemy's senses; do not stop us today.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ur" dir="rtl">دور رہ پائے جو ہم سے دم کہاں منزل میں ہے</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ur-Latn">dūr rah pāʾe jo ham se dam kahāṉ manzil meṉ hai</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Where is the strength in the goal to stay far from us?</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Credited to Bismil Azimabadi (1921); as quoted in Wikipedia's article (Urdu Wikipedia). Text from <a href="https://ur.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D8%B3%D8%B1%D9%81%D8%B1%D9%88%D8%B4%DB%8C_%DA%A9%DB%8C_%D8%AA%D9%85%D9%86%D8%A7" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia</a>; in the public domain in the United States.</p><p>The romanization is the Institute&#8217;s own (ALA-LC romanization of Urdu (without dots under the Arabic letters; ṭ, ḍ, ṛ for the retroflex consonants, ṉ for the nasal)).</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (10)</summary><ul><li>line 1.1 (and every refrain): 'sarfaroshī', literally 'selling one's head': readiness to give one's life.</li><li>line 1.2: 'qātil', killer; in Urdu poetry also the cruel beloved, here the colonial ruler.</li><li>line 1.3: 'rah na jānā rāh meṉ', literally 'do not remain on the way': do not fall behind or give up.</li><li>line 2.3: 'nisār', offered up as a sacrifice; the subject, 'we', is understood.</li><li>line 2.4: 'ghair', the other, the stranger: the enemy.</li><li>line 4.3: Holi is the spring festival played with coloured powder and water.</li><li>line 5.1: 'junūn', passion bordering on madness.</li><li>line 6.2: 'jān hathelī par liye', carrying one's life on one's palm: ready to lose it at any moment.</li><li>line 7.5: literally 'what breath (strength) is there in the destination, that it could stay far from us?': no goal can stay out of reach.</li><li>line 2.1 has no rhyme word in the source ('bāt-chīt' where the ghazal's '… meṉ hai' is expected); translated as it stands.</li></ul></details></section>
